course: dictionary
lesson: 16144
title: Doing things willingly
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Willingly means gladly, without being forced. When someone does something willingly, they choose to do it and are happy to.
explain: «Sam willingly helped Ava carry the heavy bags.» Nobody made Sam help. The word willingly tells you he wanted to, so it must mean gladly.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Sam gladly helped Ava carry the heavy bags.» It still makes sense, so gladly is the right meaning.
fact: Willingly means gladly, without being forced.
fact: Willingly is an adverb: it tells you how someone does an action, such as helped or gave.
fact: Common phrases are willingly help and willingly give.
fact: «Mia willingly gave her spare pencil to a new girl.»
fact: Unwillingly is the opposite: doing something because you have to, not because you want to.
exercise: «Tom willingly carried the shopping up the stairs.» What does willingly mean? | *Gladly, without being forced | Very quickly, in a race | Only after being asked three times | Slowly and with a complaint
exercise: «The kind lady willingly gave her seat to the old man.» Find and copy the adverb. | old | *willingly | seat | lady
exercise: Which phrase shows someone helping because they want to? | Help only to avoid trouble | Help under protest | Forced to help | *Willingly help
test: «The team willingly stayed late to finish the poster.» Why might the team have stayed late? | Because they were cross and sulky | *Because they wanted to help finish the poster | Because the teacher forced them to | Because the bell rang late
test: Which word means the opposite of willingly? | *Unwillingly | Happily | Gladly | Eagerly
exam: «Ben willingly shared his lunch with a hungry friend.» In this sentence, willingly means: | Angrily | Quietly | *Gladly, without being forced | Sadly
